// The trader's first night (docs/tech-spec.md §72): Gisli, your father's steersman, came home without him, and lives with your mother now. He'd put your purse to work.
At the bottom of Ulf's letter, in a hand that slopes like a deck in weather, Gisli has added his piece.
"Your father would have been proud, and then he'd have asked what they pay." # speaker: gisli
"I've a thing to put to you. The herring boats go out after the first frost, and a share in one costs twenty rings. It comes back at the end of the season with more. It always has, in thirty years, except the once." # speaker: gisli
"I won't say which year was the once. You were there." # speaker: gisli
* [Buy the share. #needs: rings 20]
  # fx: rings -20
  # fx: flag gisli_share
  You wrap twenty rings for the raven. Your father taught you never to put everything in one boat. You have one boat.
* [Keep the rings.]
  You write that you'd rather keep your rings on land this winter. Gisli writes back one word: "Sensible." You can hear how he says it.
- The raven leaves heavier or lighter, and complains either way.
-> END
